# Migration files are hashed by the migration runner: it records a sha256 of the
# file when it applies it and refuses to run if the file later differs, on the
# principle that a forward-only migration must never be edited.
#
# Git's autocrlf turns that safety check into a false alarm. On a Windows
# checkout every .sql arrives with CRLF, so the runner hashes different bytes
# from the ones the ledger recorded on Linux and reports "0001_initial was
# already applied but its contents have changed" — for a file nobody has
# touched. That blocked eleven pending fleet migrations until it was diagnosed.
#
# The superproject declares `* text=auto`, but submodules do not inherit it.
# Pinning eol=lf here makes the working copy byte-identical on every platform,
# so the checksum means what it claims to.
*.sql text eol=lf
